-- | OpenZeppelin Pausable - an emergency-stop switch for Daml templates.
--
-- The Daml translation of `Pausable.sol`. In Solidity, `Pausable` is inherited:
-- the flag is a field of the contract that it protects, and gated functions
-- carry the `whenNotPaused` modifier. The Daml analogue of that inheritance is
-- an interface instance, so this package defines the interface and the guards,
-- and the implementing template holds the flag.
--
-- Keeping the flag on the protected template is what makes the guard sound. The
-- state cannot be substituted, because it is not a separate contract that a
-- caller passes in; it is a field of the very contract the caller is exercising.
-- No extra fetch, no extra disclosure, and no extra authority delegation.
--
-- # One interface, no authorization
--
-- Every operation in Solidity's `Pausable` is `internal` or a view: the
-- component ships no access control, and the deriving contract wires `_pause`
-- to `Ownable`, to `AccessControl`, or to whatever else it uses. This module
-- keeps that boundary. `Pausable` carries the flag, the getter, the guards, and
-- the guarded flips, and it names no authority at all. The consumer writes the
-- choice that decides who may flip the switch.
--
-- The boundary is forced, not chosen. A Daml controller expression is pure, so
-- it cannot fetch a role grant to decide who may act, and a single `Party`
-- field cannot express a role, an M-of-N approval, or a timelock. Authorization
-- of that kind lives in a choice body, which means the choice must take the
-- caller and its credential as arguments - and only the consumer can write it:
--
-- > choice TokenRules_Pause : ContractId TokenRules
-- >   with caller : Party; grantCid : ContractId RoleGrant
-- >   controller caller
-- >   do
-- >     grant <- fetch grantCid
-- >     requireRole caller "PAUSER_ROLE" admin grant
-- >     pause this
--
-- A consumer whose pause authority really is one party writes the same choice
-- with that party as the controller and no credential argument. The pause
-- authority need not be a signatory or an observer of the implementing
-- template: the successor preserves the signatory set, so the choice already
-- carries every authority the create needs. Note that a controller is an
-- informee of the exercise node, so a non-stakeholder authority sees the
-- template payload at flip time.
--
-- # Solidity correspondence
--
-- | `Pausable.sol`        | This module                                    |
-- |-----------------------|------------------------------------------------|
-- | `bool _paused`        | `PausableView.paused`                          |
-- | `paused()`            | `isPaused`, or the interface view              |
-- | `whenNotPaused`       | `whenNotPaused`                                |
-- | `whenPaused`          | `whenPaused`                                   |
-- | `_pause()`            | `pause`, `markPaused`                          |
-- | `_unpause()`          | `unpause`, `markUnpaused`                      |
-- | `EnforcedPause()`     | `eEnforcedPause`                               |
-- | `ExpectedPause()`     | `eExpectedPause`                               |
-- | `Paused`/`Unpaused`   | the exercise node in the transaction tree      |
--
-- Solidity emits `Paused(account)` and `Unpaused(account)` because storage
-- writes are otherwise invisible. Daml needs no such event: the consumer's
-- pause choice is itself a node in the transaction tree, recorded with its
-- actor and timestamp and delivered to every stakeholder.
--
-- # Scope
--
-- This is a switch per contract. Pausing several templates at once means one
-- flag on each, or a single contract that all the protected operations are
-- exercised on.
--
-- Pause is origination control. A gated choice refuses to start while paused;
-- transactions already committed are unaffected, and a flip rejects the
-- concurrent operations that were reading the contract.
--
-- A rejected transaction commits nothing, so the ledger records the pause state
-- over an interval - bounded by the create and archive of a contract whose flag
-- is set - and not the individual operations that were refused.
--
-- CIP-0112's `pauseInfo` fields are deliberately absent. A registry that must
-- serve `reason` and `until` on its metadata endpoint carries them as its own
-- template fields beside `paused` and sets them on the value that `markPaused`
-- returns, so the whole response still comes from one on-ledger contract without freezing those
-- fields into this interface.
--
-- # Limits this module cannot enforce
--
-- Four properties are the implementer's responsibility, and none of them is
-- checked by the compiler:
--
-- 1. No Daml construct forces a gated choice to call `whenNotPaused`. A
--    forgotten guard fails silently in the permitting direction.
-- 2. A choice that calls `markPaused` or `markUnpaused` must archive the
--    contract it runs on, either by being consuming or with `archive self`.
--    `pause` and `unpause` take `self` and archive it themselves, so they
--    carry no such obligation.
-- 3. `setPaused` must preserve every field other than the flag. The flag
--    itself is verified; the rest cannot be.
-- 4. A record update applied to the value that `markPaused` or `markUnpaused`
--    returns must leave the flag alone. The check runs on the returned value,
--    and the caller owns the `create` that follows it.
--
-- # Interfaces are frozen
--
-- Daml interfaces are not SCU-upgradeable, so this package defines one
-- interface and no templates. Implementations live in consuming packages.
module OpenZeppelin.PausableV1 where

import DA.Action (unless)
import DA.Fail (FailureCategory (..), FailureStatus (..), failWithStatus)
import qualified DA.TextMap as TextMap

-- | Whether an implementing contract is currently paused.
data PausableView = PausableView with
    paused : Bool
      -- ^ Whether the contract is currently paused.
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | The switch. Implement this on any template that has a pause flag, whatever
-- authority flips it.
--
-- The interface itself grants no authority and declares no choice. It gives the
-- implementer a uniform read for tooling, the two guards, and the guarded flips
-- - the analogues of Solidity's `internal _pause()` and `_unpause()`, callable
-- only from a choice the implementer writes.
--
-- Signatories and observers: this interface adds none. It inherits whatever the
--   implementing template defines, which is why it costs no privacy surface.
-- Controllers: none. The interface declares no choice, because a choice needs a
--   controller expression drawn from the view and the view names no party.
-- Choices: none. Every state change happens in a choice the consumer writes.
-- Disclosure and privacy expectations: the flag is visible to exactly the
--   parties who can already see the implementing contract. Nothing is fetched,
--   so no party enters the read path of a gated operation. Keep sensitive
--   detail about why a pause is in force out of the implementing template's
--   observable fields.
-- Authorization assumptions: none. The interface confers no ability to pause.
--   The flip archives the contract and creates the successor, so it needs the
--   implementing template's signatory authority. A party without that
--   authority cannot flip the flag, whatever contract its choice runs on.
--   Authorization of the flip is entirely the consumer's choice to define.
-- Archival behavior: `setPaused` is pure, and `markPaused` and `markUnpaused`
--   touch the ledger only to fail. `pause` and `unpause` archive the contract
--   whose id they are given and create the successor, so the consumer's flip
--   choice is nonconsuming. See `pause`.
-- Failure modes: none of its own. The guards fail with `eEnforcedPause` or
--   `eExpectedPause`; the flips additionally fail with `eFlagNotApplied` or
--   `eImplementerTypeMismatch` when an implementation breaks the law below.
-- Upgrade and migration assumptions: frozen for the life of the package.
--   `PausableView` gains no fields, and this interface gains no methods. A
--   breaking change ships as a sibling `-v2` package.
interface Pausable where
  viewtype PausableView

  setPaused : Bool -> Pausable
    -- ^ Return this contract's own template value with the flag set.
    --
    --   The one obligation an implementer takes on. Only the template knows its own
    --   fields, so only the template can rebuild itself; the interface body has no
    --   access to them. The implementation must return *its own* template and must
    --   preserve every other field, so that pausing changes the flag and nothing
    --   else:
    --
    --   > setPaused b = toInterface (this with paused = b)
    --
    --   The return type is `Pausable` rather than the implementing template because
    --   an interface declaration has no type variable for its implementer. Any
    --   implementing template therefore satisfies the type, and returning the wrong
    --   one fails at runtime with `eImplementerTypeMismatch`. Ignoring the argument
    --   fails with `eFlagNotApplied`.
    --
    --   Do not call this method from a choice body. `pause` and `unpause` add the
    --   guard that makes a double flip fail, verify the flag, and create the
    --   successor contract; `markPaused` and `markUnpaused` do the same without
    --   the create, for a choice that sets other fields before creating. Calling
    --   `setPaused` directly skips every one of those checks.

-- | Fail unless the contract is unpaused. The `whenNotPaused` modifier.
--
-- Accepts an implementing template or a `Pausable`, so the same guard serves a
-- consumer's gated choice and its flip choice.
--
-- > choice Vault_Withdraw : ContractId Vault
-- >   with amount : Int
-- >   controller holder
-- >   do
-- >     whenNotPaused this
-- >     create this with balance = balance - amount
whenNotPaused : (HasToInterface t Pausable) => t -> Update ()
whenNotPaused x = unless (not (isPaused x)) (failWithStatus eEnforcedPause)

-- | Fail unless the contract is paused. The `whenPaused` modifier.
-- Use it on recovery choices that may run only during an incident.
whenPaused : (HasToInterface t Pausable) => t -> Update ()
whenPaused x = unless (isPaused x) (failWithStatus eExpectedPause)

-- | `paused()` as a pure function, for a choice that branches on the flag
-- rather than refusing to run.
isPaused : (HasToInterface t Pausable) => t -> Bool
isPaused x = (view (toInterface @Pausable x)).paused

-- | Pause the contract. The `_pause()` analogue: it carries no authorization,
-- so call it from a choice whose controller and body express your own pause
-- authority. It archives the contract at `self` and creates the successor, so
-- the calling choice is `nonconsuming`. Fails with `eEnforcedPause` if the
-- contract is already paused.
--
-- > nonconsuming choice Vault_Pause : ContractId Vault
-- >   controller admin
-- >   do
-- >     pause self this
--
-- Both arguments must name the contract the choice runs on. A consuming
-- calling choice fails, because the contract is already archived when `pause`
-- archives it again; that failure is deliberate, so that a flip can never
-- leave the unpaused contract live beside its paused copy.
pause : ( HasToInterface t Pausable, HasFromInterface t Pausable
        , HasArchive t, HasCreate t )
     => ContractId t -> t -> Update (ContractId t)
pause self x = do
  y <- markPaused x
  archive self
  create y

-- | Guard, then return the template value with the flag set, without creating
-- it. Use this when the successor must carry other field changes as well: set
-- them with a record update and create the result yourself, in the same
-- transaction and with no intermediate contract.
--
-- A registry that must serve CIP-0112's `pauseInfo` records the reason as it
-- pauses:
--
-- > nonconsuming choice Registry_Pause : ContractId Registry
-- >   with reason : Optional Text
-- >   controller admin
-- >   do
-- >     r <- markPaused this
-- >     archive self
-- >     create r with pauseReason = reason
--
-- CAUTION: unlike `pause`, this archives nothing, because the caller owns the
-- create. The calling choice must archive the contract it runs on, with
-- `archive self` as above or by being consuming; otherwise the unpaused
-- contract stays live beside its paused copy, with no error. The flag is
-- verified on the returned value; a record update that clears it again is the
-- caller's own error, and nothing checks it. The update must also leave alone
-- every field that determines the signatories or the observers: a changed
-- signatory field makes the create fail for lack of authority, and a dropped
-- observer silently narrows who sees the paused contract.
--
-- Fails with `eEnforcedPause` if already paused, `eFlagNotApplied` if the
-- implementation did not set the flag, or `eImplementerTypeMismatch` if
-- `setPaused` returned another template.
markPaused : (HasToInterface t Pausable, HasFromInterface t Pausable)
           => t -> Update t
markPaused x = do
  whenNotPaused x
  y <- flipTo True x
  unless (isPaused y) (failWithStatus eFlagNotApplied)
  pure y

-- | Unpause the contract. The `_unpause()` analogue: it archives the contract
-- at `self` and creates the successor, from a `nonconsuming` choice exactly as
-- `pause`. Fails with `eExpectedPause` if the contract is not paused.
unpause : ( HasToInterface t Pausable, HasFromInterface t Pausable
          , HasArchive t, HasCreate t )
        => ContractId t -> t -> Update (ContractId t)
unpause self x = do
  y <- markUnpaused x
  archive self
  create y

-- | Guard, then return the template value with the flag cleared, without
-- creating it. The counterpart of `markPaused`, for example to clear the
-- fields that recorded why the pause was in force:
--
-- > r <- markUnpaused this
-- > archive self
-- > create r with pauseReason = None
--
-- The same caution as `markPaused`: the calling choice must archive the
-- contract it runs on.
--
-- Fails with `eExpectedPause` if not paused, `eFlagNotApplied` if the
-- implementation did not clear the flag, or `eImplementerTypeMismatch` if
-- `setPaused` returned another template.
markUnpaused : (HasToInterface t Pausable, HasFromInterface t Pausable)
             => t -> Update t
markUnpaused x = do
  whenPaused x
  y <- flipTo False x
  unless (not (isPaused y)) (failWithStatus eFlagNotApplied)
  pure y

-- Call `setPaused` and convert the result back to the implementing template.
flipTo : (HasToInterface t Pausable, HasFromInterface t Pausable)
       => Bool -> t -> Update t
flipTo b x = case fromInterface (setPaused (toInterface @Pausable x) b) of
  Some y -> pure y
  None -> failWithStatus eImplementerTypeMismatch

-- Failure statuses. The Solidity custom errors, plus the two that report an
-- implementation breaking the `setPaused` law. Each is a `FailureStatus`
-- raised with `failWithStatus`, so a rejected submission reaches the Ledger
-- API as `DAML_FAILURE` with the `errorId` below, and a client matches on the
-- id rather than on the message text. In Daml Script, `trySubmit` returns it
-- as `FailureStatusError`, and `tryFailureStatus` returns it directly.

-- | Raised when an operation requires the contract to be unpaused.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/pausable-enforced-pause`.
eEnforcedPause : FailureStatus
eEnforcedPause = pausableFailure "enforced-pause" InvalidGivenCurrentSystemStateOther
  "Pausable: the contract is paused"

-- | Raised when an operation requires the contract to be paused.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/pausable-expected-pause`.
eExpectedPause : FailureStatus
eExpectedPause = pausableFailure "expected-pause" InvalidGivenCurrentSystemStateOther
  "Pausable: the contract is not paused"

-- | Raised when a flip did not leave the flag in the state it asked for,
-- because `setPaused` ignored its argument or set a field the view does not
-- read. It exists so that a mis-wired implementation
-- fails loudly instead of yielding a pause that silently does not pause, which
-- is the fail-open direction. It cannot fire against a correct implementation.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/pausable-flag-not-applied`.
eFlagNotApplied : FailureStatus
eFlagNotApplied = pausableFailure "flag-not-applied" InvalidIndependentOfSystemState
  "Pausable: the flip did not change the paused flag"

-- | Raised when `setPaused` returned an implementing template other than
-- its own.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/pausable-implementer-type-mismatch`.
eImplementerTypeMismatch : FailureStatus
eImplementerTypeMismatch = pausableFailure "implementer-type-mismatch"
  InvalidIndependentOfSystemState
  "Pausable: setPaused did not return the implementing template"

pausableFailure : Text -> FailureCategory -> Text -> FailureStatus
pausableFailure name category message = FailureStatus with
  errorId = "openzeppelin.com/pausable-" <> name
  category
  message
  meta = TextMap.empty
